    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Gunnar Henderson** and **Jacob Wilson** headlining the lineup as the clear tactical pillars. **Gunnar Henderson** (76.16) benefits significantly from the matchup against Freddy Peralta; while the park factor is slightly suppressive, the combination of a high-leverage lineup spot and Peralta\u2019s slow delivery offers a rare chance to capitalize on stolen base upside, a critical driver in our 5x5 strategy. Similarly, **Jacob Wilson** (74.08) commands a premium start today. He draws a highly favorable matchup against Bradford, supported by a 19% boost from the opposing SP\u2019s xERA and a 10% park factor bump. **Jackson Merrill** (69.57) rounds out the top-tier plays, leveraging his dynamic platoon advantage against Bibee to maintain elite efficiency in the 5-hole.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today's roster card involved the final outfield spot and our bench utility. **Coby Mayo** (43.08) was the closest exclusion, showing respectable raw numbers against Peralta. However, he was relegated to the bench because his lower lineup position and negative platoon impact against a high-skill arm lowered his projected efficiency compared to the starters. We are also monitoring the constraints on our depth; with **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** currently on the IL, our margin for error is razor-thin. We are carefully avoiding **Luis Lara** (39.29), whose matchup against the elite arm of Skubal dropped his efficiency below our 40.0 floor, ensuring we preserve our limited game caps for more opportunistic windows.\n\nRisk management is central to today\u2019s strategy, particularly as we navigate several high-skill opposing arms. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (56.52) and **Andy Pages** (47.54) are facing Cease and Henderson, respectively\u2014two pitchers with sub-3.00 xERAs. While these starters remain in the lineup due to their dynamic platoon profiles and strong order positioning, the downward pressure from the opposing SP skill requires us to keep a close eye on early-inning outcomes. We are also relying on the platoon and BvP metrics to sustain **Miguel Vargas** (44.43), as his matchup against Anderson provides enough of a statistical tailwind to keep him above the benching threshold, though he remains a marginal play that could be pulled if momentum shifts early.",
